Here is a list of the main points from the text:

* The authors present a computational framework for finding Approximate Functional Dependencies (AFDs) in any time fashion.
* They formalize two core sub-problems: maximum and minimum error estimates, which calculate the upper and lower bounds of an AFD's error based on data that is read.
* The authors propose a suite of algorithms to solve these bound estimation problems.
* They present exact and approximate lattice-based algorithms for finding anytime AFDs using these bounds.
* The framework has an anytime property, meaning it can provide effective results even after reading only a small portion of the data.
* The authors conduct experiments to demonstrate the effectiveness of their algorithms under different data organization strategies.
* Future work directions include considering partial/full marginals with known errors and designing approximate but faster approaches using Dynamic Programming or other techniques.
